WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.084 --> 00:00:02.585
Take a look at this baseball team.

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:05.463
They're the 2022 World Series winners.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:06.881
The Houston Astros.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.383
“...Kyle Tucker.”

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:10.719
“This time they finished the job!”

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:13.054
More than half the players 
on this team

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:14.305
aren't from the United States.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.434
13 of them in this photo 
are from Latin America.

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:19.686
But one country in particular 

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.938
stands out with six players 
on this team...

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:23.982
The Dominican Republic.

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:27.152
In fact, a number of Dominican players 

00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:28.778
are on some of the best teams 

00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:30.864
in Major League Baseball 
across the country.

00:00:32.157 --> 00:00:34.868
Overall, they make up more than 10%

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.661
of all players in the league.

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:39.372
By far the most in the pool 
of foreign born players.

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:43.126
So what's so special about baseball 
in the DR?

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.629
And why do so many of its players 
end up in the US?

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:57.766
“Yes, there's no doubt about it.”

00:00:58.099 --> 00:00:59.476
“Baseball is king.”

00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:02.771
Baseball has a very long history in the US.

00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:05.148
A college student scribbled something 

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:08.109
about playing “baste ball” 
way back in 1786...

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:11.988
and that's considered the first mention 
of the game in the country.

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:15.533
Over time, college campuses 
became home to baseball...

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:19.120
while professional teams started 
shaping up across many states.

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.582
By 1901, a couple of wealthy Americans

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:25.376
organized these teams into 
two professional leagues:

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.879
the National League and the American League.

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:30.882
Together, they would eventually 
become known as

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:33.843
Major League Baseball 
or the MLB.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:38.890
Since the 1900s, teams have been 
playing against each other

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.893
until the best from each league 
ends up in the World Series.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.021
The first World Series 
was a hit right away.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:47.982
It brought in over 100,000 attendees...

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.110
and tens of thousands of dollars.

00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:53.530
Over the next few years...

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:56.032
millions of fans bought tickets 
to these games...

00:01:56.991 --> 00:01:58.993
and baseball executives started building

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:01.287
massive stadiums to make 
room for them.

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:03.581
By the early 20th century...

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:06.292
baseball became a very profitable industry.

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:10.797
And it became known as 
America's favorite pastime.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.884
But during all that time, 
baseball was becoming

00:02:14.884 --> 00:02:17.595
the favorite pastime over here 
in the DR, too.

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.140
And it got there 
via Cuba.

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:25.520
The game first arrived here 
in Havana in the 1860s...

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.482
mostly through elite Cuban students 
who attended college in the US...

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:31.484
and brought the game 
back home with them.

00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:35.864
Some of them set up teams 
that started playing each other.

00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:40.201
Eventually, the sport spread through 
affluent Cubans in Havana...

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.537
to the working class 
around the country.

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:44.289
If it’s a company...

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:48.168
and the owner of the company 
is the one playing the game

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:51.379
they might encourage their workers 
to play the game as well

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:53.131
so that a game can be played.

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:55.842
For the game to become 
a national entity...

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:59.053
it has to be rooted in 
the working class.

00:03:00.388 --> 00:03:01.389
Over the years

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:04.267
baseball became such a big part 
of the country's identity...

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.144
that Cubans took 
their favorite sport

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:07.812
with them wherever they went.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.940
That happened in the 1860s...

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:13.693
when many slaves revolted 
against the wealthy...

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:16.029
especially sugar plantation owners

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:18.823
who fled in search of a new place 
to set up shop.

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:20.617
They ended up nearby...

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:22.744
in the Dominican Republic.

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:24.996
And they brought baseball 
with them.

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:26.706
Once again

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.626
the sport first took off with 
an affluent crowd in the capital...

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.170
before taking hold over 
the workers in the east...

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.673
especially the city of
San Pedro de Macorís

00:03:35.673 --> 00:03:38.509
where Cuban ex-pats had set up 
their sugar refineries.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.722
What these refinery managers 
and owners did

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:46.059
was they created incentives 
for their workers.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:51.314
Namely, that if you can beat 
a nearby refinery next Saturday

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:52.899
well, you just don't have to work.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.903
Games between refineries here 
became huge spectacles...

00:03:57.195 --> 00:03:59.405
and a pastime for Dominicans everywhere.

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:01.991
When you have the economic incentive...

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.744
wedded to a tremendous...

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.330
fan base and passion...

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:12.085
so that after the game, people argue
about who played what...

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:14.629
and who was excellent, 
who wasn't

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:17.215
they’re creating a baseball culture.

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:20.051
Back in the US and Cuba

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.552
baseball continued to thrive.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:23.888
Teams in the US

00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:26.140
played more and more games 
throughout the country...

00:04:26.808 --> 00:04:29.060
making baseball a million dollar industry.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:31.813
While in Cuba

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:35.066
it became the most popular sport 
for a very different reason.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.360
For many, many years

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.571
 when Spanish colonial rulers 
tried to squash Cuban identity...

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:42.699
including their love of baseball

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:44.075
Cubans pushed back.

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:45.702
Instead of giving up the sport...

00:04:46.035 --> 00:04:49.122
they turned it into a symbol of
pride and nationalism.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.543
After the Spanish-American War, 
Spain left Cuba 

00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:56.045
and the US occupied the country 
for four years.

00:04:56.671 --> 00:05:00.008
That's when Americans learned more 
about Cuban baseball talent...

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.928
and the MLB soon realized 
they could get skilled players

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:06.389
on cheaper contracts than 
some American players.

00:05:07.015 --> 00:05:09.684
So they started signing Cuban players.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:11.853
By the late 1950s...

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.480
the MLB had signed 49 Cubans 

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.482
and players like Minnie Miñoso 

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.360
and Luis Alomá had become 
household names.

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:24.490
But after decades of US military 
interventions in Cuba...

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.827
the two countries broke relations in 1961...

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:31.956
after the Cuban Revolution and 
Fidel Castro's rise to power.

00:05:32.206 --> 00:05:35.084
“This was the scene of turmoil
in the capital of Havana...

00:05:35.293 --> 00:05:37.420
as the climax of revolution was reached.”

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.965
That put the MLB's entire operation at risk...

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:44.010
because they couldn't rely on 
Cuban talent anymore.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:50.266
There had to be something monumental 
that broke that trend.

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.602
Once that trade embargo happened...

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:56.731
then that talent had to be 
picked up from elsewhere

00:05:56.731 --> 00:05:59.275
and that was an opening 
for the Dominican Republic.

00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:01.736
No question that that played a big role.

00:06:02.653 --> 00:06:05.740
The US and the DR had a very rough
relationship at first.

00:06:06.657 --> 00:06:08.201
In the early 20th century

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.745
The DR owed a significant 
amount of money to Europe...

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:14.957
and that created the possibility of 
a European military intervention.

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:19.170
But the US didn't want European forces 
near its borders...

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:21.881
so it sent troops to occupy the DR.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.635
The occupation was brutal 
and lasted eight years.

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.679
But something unexpected happened 
during the occupation.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.558
Dominicans played baseball games 
against the US troops...

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:34.143
and often beat them.

00:06:36.187 --> 00:06:37.605
Many of the local players

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:40.274
especially around the 
San Pedro Sugar Mills area...

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:42.527
where a lot of the fighting took place

00:06:42.527 --> 00:06:43.820
were against the occupation.

00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:46.155
So these games took on 
a new meaning.

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:50.118
What is interesting is that 

00:06:50.118 --> 00:06:53.413
the game is not so much 
beating the Americans...

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:56.290
but that as a result of 
beating the Americans

00:06:56.707 --> 00:06:59.085
they get more pride 
in their own game.

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:02.213
By the 1930s, after the occupation ended...

00:07:02.547 --> 00:07:04.841
Professional teams in the DR
were getting bigger.

00:07:05.258 --> 00:07:06.342
And it became an arena

00:07:06.342 --> 00:07:08.052
where players from the sugar mill teams...

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.721
could showcase their talents 
on a national stage.

00:07:11.556 --> 00:07:13.391
But even the best Dominican players

00:07:13.391 --> 00:07:15.476
many of whom were of 
black African descent...

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:17.770
didn't end up with the MLB at that time.

00:07:18.062 --> 00:07:20.982
Because in the US, baseball leagues 
were segregated.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.901
So instead of trying 
to make it in the US...

00:07:24.068 --> 00:07:25.695
the professional league in the DR

00:07:25.695 --> 00:07:28.239
brought over talented American baseball players

00:07:28.239 --> 00:07:31.117
from the Negro League who were 
also left out of the MLB.

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:34.328
Players from this league ended up 
playing in the DR

00:07:34.328 --> 00:07:36.539
during their offseasons for extra income...

00:07:37.331 --> 00:07:38.374
like Satchel Paige

00:07:38.374 --> 00:07:39.834
who was paid thousands of dollars

00:07:39.834 --> 00:07:43.004
for playing on Dominican dictator
Rafael Trujillo’s

00:07:43.004 --> 00:07:43.963
professional team.

00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:47.258
These Dominican professional league games

00:07:47.258 --> 00:07:49.927
drew thousands of spectators 
who wanted to watch

00:07:49.927 --> 00:07:52.847
Negro League players, as well as the 
Dominican players

00:07:52.847 --> 00:07:54.807
who are now receiving more attention.

00:07:56.058 --> 00:07:57.727
So by the late 1950s

00:07:57.727 --> 00:08:00.229
after the desegregation of baseball 
in the US...

00:08:00.521 --> 00:08:02.982
the MLB turned its full attention to the DR.

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:05.693
They found a new pipeline for talent.

00:08:06.277 --> 00:08:09.238
It was an extension of 
the neocolonial relation.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.366
Major League Baseball was finding cheap resources...

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:16.037
signing it on the cheap, 
taking it to elsewhere

00:08:16.037 --> 00:08:18.748
to get refined and consumed
in another place.

00:08:19.373 --> 00:08:22.210
By the 1960s, the MLB 
was expanding quickly.

00:08:22.543 --> 00:08:25.421
They added more teams that meant 
they needed more players.

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:29.217
They signed outstanding Dominican players 
like Ozzie Virgil

00:08:29.217 --> 00:08:30.968
the Alou Brothers and 
Juan Marichal...

00:08:31.219 --> 00:08:33.554
who brought thousands of fans 
to the MLB games.

00:08:34.764 --> 00:08:37.391
Soon, the MLB wanted more 
Dominican players...

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:38.935
but they quickly realized

00:08:38.935 --> 00:08:40.895
they needed local scouts to spot talent.

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:43.481
That's where popular scouts like

00:08:43.481 --> 00:08:45.650
Epy Guerrero and Ralph Avila came in.

00:08:46.484 --> 00:08:50.238
Epy created the country's first 
baseball academy in the 1970s

00:08:50.238 --> 00:08:51.447
to train young talent.

00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:55.618
Then in 1987, Ralph created an academy

00:08:55.618 --> 00:08:58.579
that was directly linked to 
an MLB team in the US:

00:08:58.996 --> 00:09:00.414
the Los Angeles Dodgers.

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.751
The LA team invested millions 
in this academy.

00:09:04.627 --> 00:09:07.046
Together, they created a formal process

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.757
for Dominican players to end up 
on MLB teams.

00:09:10.258 --> 00:09:11.676
It goes something like this:

00:09:12.635 --> 00:09:15.763
Dominican scouts identify new talent 
throughout the country.

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:19.725
Then they hold tryouts where 
players showcase their skills.

00:09:20.142 --> 00:09:21.477
The most impressive players

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:23.479
get a contract to train 
with the academy.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:25.898
If a player makes it through training...

00:09:26.148 --> 00:09:28.109
they either sign a professional contract

00:09:28.109 --> 00:09:30.820
to continue training with a 
minor league team in the US...

00:09:31.445 --> 00:09:33.406
or make it to an MLB team.

00:09:34.448 --> 00:09:36.492
Only a small percentage of the trainees

00:09:36.492 --> 00:09:38.452
actually achieve the MLB dream.

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:41.747
But that's what happened with 
former pitcher Ramone Martinez...

00:09:41.998 --> 00:09:45.293
who went straight from the academy 
to playing for the LA Dodgers.

00:09:45.835 --> 00:09:47.753
“And Ramone Martinez

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:50.381
no hitting the Marlins
seven to nothing.”

00:09:50.965 --> 00:09:54.051
Soon, other big teams set up academies 
that help them spot

00:09:54.051 --> 00:09:56.512
and train major players 
like Robinson Canó

00:09:56.512 --> 00:09:59.348
and Albert Pujols, who eventually 
became All-Stars.

00:10:00.224 --> 00:10:02.935
This pipeline of talent worked
so well for the MLB

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:05.354
that there are now 30 academies 
in the DR.

00:10:05.605 --> 00:10:08.441
One for each of the 30 MLB teams 
in the US.

00:10:09.734 --> 00:10:11.777
Since the beginning of the academy system...

00:10:12.028 --> 00:10:14.739
the MLB has signed hundreds 
of Dominican players.

00:10:16.616 --> 00:10:18.659
And the leading cities for this talent pipeline

00:10:18.659 --> 00:10:20.911
are the same as the ones 
where the sugar mill games

00:10:20.911 --> 00:10:22.038
were once played.

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:24.165
With San Pedro right at the top.

00:10:26.792 --> 00:10:28.753
The system has created an opportunity

00:10:28.753 --> 00:10:31.505
for top Dominican players to end up 
with the MLB.

00:10:32.423 --> 00:10:33.883
And they make millions now.

00:10:34.175 --> 00:10:36.761
But no matter how far 
the Dominican players go...

00:10:37.094 --> 00:10:40.514
the MLB still signs them for less 
than some American players.

00:10:41.474 --> 00:10:42.933
So this is still a way for them

00:10:42.933 --> 00:10:44.769
to make massive profits in the US

00:10:44.769 --> 00:10:47.396
while giving foreign-born players 
cheaper contracts.

00:10:48.272 --> 00:10:51.233
The DR makes money off the academy system, too.

00:10:51.359 --> 00:10:54.320
The scouts helped negotiate 
better contracts for the players

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.031
but keep a massive cut for themselves 
at every turn.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.868
Overall, Dominican players

00:11:00.868 --> 00:11:03.913
bring in about $400 million per year 
in the Major League.

00:11:04.163 --> 00:11:05.831
And they send about half of that home.

00:11:06.707 --> 00:11:11.379
Plus, the academies bring in about 
$125 million a year, create more jobs...

00:11:11.629 --> 00:11:14.048
and the MLB spends millions maintaining them.

00:11:15.216 --> 00:11:19.011
There's no question that after 
agriculture and tourism

00:11:19.011 --> 00:11:23.182
baseball has become the third leading 
source of revenue

00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:24.642
for the country as a whole.

00:11:25.726 --> 00:11:29.105
Before the Dominican players who helped 
generate so much of their profit...

00:11:29.563 --> 00:11:31.232
the system has a long way to go.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.820
Some Dominican All-Stars are starting
to push for that change.

00:11:36.487 --> 00:11:39.699
They're using their fame in the MLB 
to ask for a system

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:42.284
that works equally for players 
in both countries.

00:11:43.327 --> 00:11:47.081
To them, baseball is as much Dominican 
as it is American.

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:50.292
They are so passionate about the game.

00:11:50.668 --> 00:11:52.795
They have such a deep history for the game.

00:11:53.421 --> 00:11:54.755
An understanding of the game.

00:11:56.549 --> 00:11:58.634
They're actually in positions 
of control.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.346
So it's a battle between 
who controls the game.

00:12:05.766 --> 00:12:06.517
Hey everyone.

00:12:06.517 --> 00:12:09.270
I'm Rajaa, the producer of 
this episode of Vox Atlas.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.021
Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:12.732
There's so much we couldn't get to

00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:14.400
but this video is just a start.

00:12:14.692 --> 00:12:15.901
If you want to learn more about

00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:17.653
what players go through 
in these academies...

00:12:17.862 --> 00:12:21.157
we added a couple of great resources 
in our description as well.

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.492
Our goal with Atlas and 
all of our coverage

00:12:23.492 --> 00:12:25.828
 is to help you understand 
the big global topics.

00:12:26.078 --> 00:12:28.038
Which is why we want to keep 
our work for free.

00:12:28.622 --> 00:12:31.041
But advertising isn't enough 
to support all the work

00:12:31.041 --> 00:12:32.168
that goes into Atlas.

00:12:32.501 --> 00:12:35.963
If you really like our videos, 
head to Vox.com/give-now

00:12:35.963 --> 00:12:38.591
to chip in and keep our work 
free for everyone.

